      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e In this case, the vcpu-\u003earch.cpuid_entries is NULL. Then when pVM will read 0 for its CPUID leaves. So there is even no APIC feature. Such CPUID is definitely abnormal. But it is still a bit different with directly using the CPUID entries from the untrusted host without enforcement, as the host may set some feature bit (which is actually 0 in the native CPUID) in the pVM\u0027s CPUID entries to let the pVM bypass some necessary mitigations to a CPU bug. So I am not sure if there is any security issues with all 0 CPUID. It seems the pVM itself can also easily detect such abnormal CPUID and refuse to run. And we can also prevent the vCPU to run if vcpu-\u003earch.cpuid_entries \u003d\u003d NULL, but then having CPUID will be a force requirement for all kinds of pVM\u0027s OS, e.g., we cannot run a quite tiny/simple pVM which doesn\u0027t rely on any CPUID.\n\nYeah, makes sense.\n\n\u003e We can do that. But as kvm_apic_set_base() relies on the vcpu-\u003earch.cpu_caps having X2APIC feature and vcpu-\u003earch.maxphyaddr, we will need manually call guest_cpu_cap_set(vcpu, X86_FEATURE_X2APIC) and kvm_vcpu_after_set_cpuid(vcpu) in __vcpu_create() before calling kvm_apic_set_base(). Calling kvm_vcpu_after_set_cpuid(vcpu) is fine as it is reasonable to set vcpu with empty CPUID entries, and this is also what the kvm_arch_vcpu_create() done. Calling guest_cpu_cap_set(vcpu, X86_FEATURE_X2APIC) is a bit tricky, as we set a guest cpu cap without respecting its CPUID. But as we will enforce the CPUID later, it seems fine.\n\nMakes sense. Shall we do that?\n\nActually I\u0027m fine with setting x2APIC here in pkvm_vcpu_after_set_cpuid(), not at vCPU creation, as long as it is still correct and secure. And now it seems like it should be ok: if the host never calls after_set_cpuid, there is no CPUID, so APIC support is not advertised, so the pVM will not even use APIC anyway?\n\nOr are there are security or functionality reasons why we should still enforce enabling x2APIC even if the pVM does not use APIC at all?\n\nBTW this is not just a theoretical scenario for us, since, as I\u0027ve just found out, Trusty doesn\u0027t currently use APIC, it only uses PIC instead (as it doesn\u0027t support SMP on x86 yet), and it looks like Trusty will boot successfully without CPUID.\n\n\u003e For the host, maybe can keep the current implementation which is to override apic base after the vcpu is reset by calling guest_cpu_cap_set(vcpu, X86_FEATURE_X2APIC), and then do a KVM_BUG_ON(guest_cpu_cap_has(vcpu, X86_FEATURE_X2APIC), vcpu-\u003ekvm) check in the pkvm_vcpu_after_set_cpuid to make sure the updated cpu caps still has the X86_FEATURE_X2APIC bit?\n\nMakes sense.\n\nI\u0027m fine with whatever works correctly.\n\nMaybe just for mere consistency, if we enforce x2APIC in pKVM after setting CPUID, not on vCPU creation/reset, then it makes sense to do that after setting CPUID in the host as well; while if we enforce x2APIC in pKVM on vCPU creation/reset, it makes sense to do that on vCPU creation/reset in the host as well?",
      "parentUuid": "3d8e3f7f_1a033194",
      "revId": "863f921b11605d0568635b675b4dea50b7c52825",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
