      "writtenOn": "2026-03-12T03:13:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Makes sense. Shall we do that?\n\nYeah, let\u0027s do that.  Now I prefer to enforce x2apic mode on vCPU creation/reset, which is a bit simpler than doing it in pkvm_after_vcpu_set_cpuid, to avoid restoring the cpuid.\n\n\u003e Or are there are security or functionality reasons why we should still enforce enabling x2APIC even if the pVM does not use APIC at all?\nBTW this is not just a theoretical scenario for us, since, as I\u0027ve just found out, Trusty doesn\u0027t currently use APIC, it only uses PIC instead (as it doesn\u0027t support SMP on x86 yet), and it looks like Trusty will boot successfully without CPUID.\n\nHmm, if the pVM doesn\u0027t use APIC, it means the pVM won\u0027t set TPR and the TPR is 0, which means allowing the class 1 interrupts. So with virtual interrupt delivery enabled, the host can still inject exception vectors 16 ~ 31. To mitigate this, the pVM should always configure TPR as 0x10 even it doesn\u0027t use APIC, or the pKVM hypervisor sets TPR as 0x10 after reset to enforce this, which seems a better solution. If this makes sense to you, I will make change in the pKVM hypervisor to set TPR as 0x10.\n\n\u003e Maybe just for mere consistency, if we enforce x2APIC in pKVM after setting CPUID, not on vCPU creation/reset, then it makes sense to do that after setting CPUID in the host as well; while if we enforce x2APIC in pKVM on vCPU creation/reset, it makes sense to do that on vCPU creation/reset in the host as well?\n\nYeah, makes sense.",
      "parentUuid": "d262469b_75124b00",
